= Machine learning — Technical =

== Learning problem ==
A supervised learning problem can be represented by:

<pre>
data D = {(x_i, y_i)}
hypothesis/model family H
loss function ℓ
learning algorithm A
</pre>

The algorithm produces:

<pre>
h = A(D)
</pre>

with the goal of minimizing expected loss on a target distribution, not merely empirical loss on D.

== Empirical and expected risk ==
For empirical risk:

<pre>
R_emp(h) = (1/n) Σ_i ℓ(h(x_i), y_i)
</pre>

Generalization concerns the relationship between empirical risk and expected target risk.

== Paradigms ==
Machine learning includes supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised, self-supervised, reinforcement, online, active, transfer, and generative learning.

These paradigms differ in what feedback/data are available and what objective is optimized.

== Inductive structure ==
Generalization requires assumptions beyond finite observations.

Those assumptions appear through hypothesis classes, priors, regularization, architecture, optimization dynamics, augmentations, invariances, and data construction.

